  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nPrimary forecast concern today is obviously the winter storm\npotential tomorrow afternoon into Thanksgiving Day. A sharp\nthermal gradient is present across the lowest 400mb of the\natmosphere or so ahead of a sharp trough digging into the\nsouthern Plains this afternoon. As that trough continues to dig\nlow level pressures/heights fall in that baroclinic zone and\nflow starts to move from the southeast across the gradient\nleading to warm air advective ascent and frontogenetic forcing that\nshould begin producing precipitation by tomorrow afternoon. A \nsecondary upper low then moves over the area through Wednesday \nnight which strengthens 700-850mb Fgen forcing with large jet \nlevel ascent from a jet max escaping to the north out of the \nlow. Surface flow strengthens out of the northwest pulling in \ncooler and drier air into the lowest couple hundred mb of the \natmosphere. The end result is a large and sustained area of \nlift. There will be a rain/snow line somewhere over the quad-\nstate. There will likely be dynamical cooling associated with \nthe intense upper low as well and that is indicated in \ndeterministic guidance. \n\nThe end result of all this is intense snowfall rates are likely\nenough to prepare for Wednesday evening into Thursday morning \nover parts of SEMO and southern Illinois. With convective \nbanding snowfall possible invof and just ahead of the upper low \nthat swirls in place over or near the CWA through Thursday \nmorning. The rain/snow delineation is somewhat low confidence \nand assumes the temperature forecast in the global and high res \nmodels is reasonably representative. This is a very complex and \nsomewhat climatologically anomalous event however and placement \nconfidence is lower than normal and lead time on the winter \nstorm watch is significantly less than normal. There is a higher\nchance than normal that parts of the winter storm watch area \nreceive all rain if the warm advection is stronger. It should \nalso be pointed out if the dynamical cooling/convective \ninstability is stronger the top of this storm is also higher, in\nthe 6-8 inch range in parts of southwest IL and northern SEMO. \n\nThe 25th percentile with this storm is 2-3 inches in Perry\nCounty, MO dropping to about 1.5 inches along I-57 in southern\nIllinois, while the 75th percentile is 6-8 inches from about\nCarbondale to Mt. Vernon, IL and points west. The probability \nfor impactful snow (2 inches or more) is about 85% in the watch \narea, and 4 inches or more is about 60% in the watch area. The \ncutoff will likely be sharp although it is possible a buffer \nwinter weather advisory area may be required as you stretch \nfurther east into southwest Indiana as the event nears with \nabout a 30-40% chance of 2 inches or more there.\n\nOne positive factor for impacts is that subground temperatures\nare about 44-47 degrees. Fast snowfall rates will overcome warm\nground temperatures and impacts to untreated roadways will begin\nquickly, but the marginal air temperatures warming after precip\nends, and subground temperatures should make for fairly fast\nmelting/clean up keeping the duration of the impacts fairly\nshort. By Friday highs work into the upper 40s or low 50s and\nthat should end any impacts.\n\nSunday into Tuesday another storm system is possible that we end\nup on the warmer side of. The ECMWF is much faster with this\nthan the GFS but both solutions look to produce some\nthunderstorm potential that we can evaluate as it gets closer.",
